The useRef hook in React creates a mutable object that persists across component re-renders without causing the component to update when its value changes. It is primarily used to directly access DOM elements or to store values that do not affect the UI. 

Example 1: Accessing and Focusing a DOM Element
A common use case for useRef is to interact with a DOM element directly, such as focusing an input field when a component mounts. 

jsx
import React, { useRef, useEffect } from 'react';

function FocusInputComponent() {
  // 1. Create a ref with an initial value of null.
  const inputRef = useRef(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    // 3. Access the DOM element using the .current property and call the focus method.
    if (inputRef.current) {
      inputRef.current.focus();
    }
  }, []); // The empty dependency array ensures this runs once when the component mounts.

  return (
    <div>
      {/* 2. Attach the ref object to the input element using the `ref` attribute. */}
      <input ref={inputRef} type="text" placeholder="I will be focused" />
    </div>
  );
}

export default FocusInputComponent;

Example 2: Storing a Mutable Value Without Causing Re-renders
useRef is useful for storing any mutable value that needs to persist across renders but shouldn't trigger an update to the user interface, like a counter or a timer ID. 

jsx
import React, { useRef, useState } from 'react';

function CounterComponent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  // Create a ref to store the number of button clicks (internal logic).
  const clickCountRef = useRef(0);

  const handleClick = () => {
    // Update the state to trigger a re-render and update the UI.
    setCount(c => c + 1);

    // Update the ref value (does not trigger a re-render).
    clickCountRef.current += 1;
    console.log("Total button clicks (internal):", clickCountRef.current);
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <p>Current UI Count: {count}</p>
      <button onClick={handleClick}>
        Click me
      </button>
      {/* The ref value is not displayed in the UI because changes to it do not re-render the component. */}
    </div>
  );
}

export default CounterComponent;

Key Use Cases

Accessing DOM elements (e.g., managing focus, media playback, triggering animations).
Storing mutable values that do not affect the UI (e.g., timer IDs, previous state values, API response caches).
Performance optimization by avoiding unnecessary re-renders for non-visual data updates. 



